cartridge "NIGHT EXPRESS/AMY & CLAUDE/2026"
' merged single-file source (main.alexis + generated art/sound), built 2026-11-17 16:34 EST America/Toronto (r12.1, --opt balanced)

' NIGHT EXPRESS - an original game by Amy & Claude for
' Amy Studio, 2026, in Amy. The thief from NIGHT ROUNDS escapes across the
' roofs of a night express; the guard chases him car by car to the engine.
'
' Controls (controller 1): stick left/right runs (it takes a few steps to
' reach full speed and to stop), UP or the RIGHT button jumps, DOWN ducks
' (and crawls), the LEFT button grabs (a lunge: 14 px in 8 frames, then 20
' frames to recover on the roof; in the air the jump goes on).
' Rules:
' - each run starts on the last coach; the thief is ahead. Reach the
'   engine (he is cornered: 200) or grab him (500) and the run is won:
'   bonus, next level (LV).
' - grabbing (v3, 2026-11-17): he sprints away from a guard close behind,
'   but he is OFF BALANCE (his stripes turn yellow, GRAB! shows when you
'   are near) for a moment after every landing (25 frames at LV 1, 1 less
'   a level) and after throwing coal; he is yellow too while he ducks or
'   throws. Only a yellow thief can be caught: a lunge that reaches him
'   (18 px from the guard's x + 4, on every frame of the lunge) catches him.
'   Off balance he moves at 1/8 of his pace and does not jump (he waits at
'   the edge). A missed lunge costs 20 frames: mashing does not pay.
' - falling between the cars costs a life (icons). When the time bar runs
'   out the thief escapes over the engine: that also costs a life.
' - low bridges and signal gantries come over the train: duck under them.
'   Coal thrown back from the tender rolls along the roof and luggage sits
'   on the roofs: jump them. Any of these knocks you down for a moment.
' - the train: 4 coaches at LV 1-2, 5 at LV 3-4, 6 at LV 5-6, 7 at LV 7,
'   8 from LV 8 (TrainCars), then the tender and the engine; "n CARS  GET
'   READY" at the start of a run, and the map in the HUD (row 2: the train,
'   the guard cyan, the thief white). Every gap is 16 or 24 px (a running
'   jump flies 32 px; the thief jumps at 20/16 px a frame at least: 41 px),
'   and the guard can reach the engine on every level's train well inside
'   its time (tools: prove.py).
' - each level: the thief is faster (his pace 11-15, never over the
'   guard's 16; his sprint 17-25), obstacles come more often, the time is
'   shorter: 38 s at LV 1, 2 s less a level, 1 s less a level after LV 8,
'   3 s more for each coach over 4: 38 36 | 37 35 | 36 34 | 35 | 36 35 34 s;
'   the rest stops rising at LV 8.
'   3 lives, one more every 2 levels (max 5).
' - LV 10 is the last run: win it and the game is over, won: 500 points for
'   each life left, dawn comes up over the line, THE END and the credits; a
'   press (after 2 s) returns to the title.
' - knocked down by luggage: pushed back up to 22 px along the roof; a jump
'   pressed while getting up is done on the first frame back up.
'
' House frame: title menu START / HOW TO PLAY / CREDITS over the running
' train (a demo chase); HOW TO PLAY pages over the demo; game over: one
' press returns to the title. Text and HUD are written in the sky (rows
' 1-7) through a buffer, one row a frame. Nothing flashes over 3 Hz.
'
' The train strip holds the longest train (8 coaches: E3, E4 (copies of
' C3, C4), E1, E2, then the 4 first ones): x is along that strip for every
' level. A level's train is its last coaches: the tender (832) and the
' engine (888) never move, the rear does (TrainL; the camera from CamMin).
' Surf, the strip's names (smoke, cab, body, wheels) and the R tables are in
' ROM; the rear end on screen (sky, then the last coach's end, 4 cells)
' comes from the strip's own first cells (NxOutT); the R tables start at the
' cell ExtraC + RSkip (every row before is alike: TrRow, row 0 below).
'
' Screen (24 rows; columns 2-29 and rows 1-22 visible, CRT safe area):
'   rows 1-7    sky, moon, stars; the text/HUD rows 1, 3, 5 (and 2, 4, 6)
'   rows 8-10   far hills, 1/4 of the ground speed
'   rows 11-12  forest and mist, 1/2
'   rows 13-14  telegraph poles, fence, bushes: full ground speed
'   rows 15-20  the train (smoke, cab, roofs, windows, wheels): the camera
'   rows 21-22  rails and ballast: full ground speed
' The ground moves 1 pixel a frame and the camera at most 1, so the near
' layer moves at most 2 pixels a frame. Bridges and gantries are drawn with
' their own tiles (NxObs): the span over the track in rows 13-15, the leg or
' pier behind the train in row 16 and its footing beside the track in row 21;
' coal in row 16. The gantry is a moonlit lattice truss over the track (rows
' 14-15) with a lamp on its mast (row 16). The only sprites are the guard (2) and the thief (2): never
' more than 4 on a line.
'
' Frame: wait for the vertical blank (the NMI runs only during the wait and
' only flags the frame: NO_NMI), then NxFrame writes the sprite table, every
' band that moved, the obstacle overlay and one dirty text row, starting at
' once in the blank; then the NMI's own work (controller, sound: NxLate),
' then the logic of the next frame. Frame budget rules (0 lost frames in the
' bots): the camera moves every other frame by the engine and while a bridge
' or gantry is on screen; in a frame where the camera crosses a cell the mid
' band waits a frame (NxLayers).

